--------------------------------------------------------------------------
What this skill does not cover

The scope is UI and UX design: decisions about what a reader receives. Several
things that sit next to those decisions were deliberately left out, because
they are decisions about implementation structure rather than about the
interface, and a skill that carries both stops being able to say which it is
enforcing.

Left out: where application state lives and how it is split; render
performance and virtualisation; how assets are produced, sized, and exported;
profiling and measurement procedure; build configuration.

None of that is unimportant. It belongs to a different skill.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
How the rules were written

The rules stand on the platform vendors' own documents — Apple's Human
Interface Guidelines, Material Design, the platforms' developer documentation
— and on WCAG 2.2. Where a document states a number, the number is taken as
stated and listed below. Where a convention depended on one platform's
controls or one framework's interfaces, what was kept is the decision
underneath it; conventions that were only about a specific runtime were left
out rather than translated. Every rule was written so that a reviewer can
count or confirm it rather than judge it.

Rules that frontend-design already covers were left out entirely rather than
paraphrased, which is why rules/anti-slop.md carries a count but no catalogue
of looks, rules/copy.md carries an inventory but no guidance on writing a
sentence, and rules/tokens.md carries a declaration but not the argument for
what belongs in it.

rules/layout.md and rules/a11y.md assume no platform. Layout conventions can
only be inherited where a platform is assumed, so the layout file states its
own. frontend-design names the accessibility floor in a single line — visible
keyboard focus, reduced motion respected, visually accessible — and carries no
procedure for reaching it, so the walks, the counts, and the rules on names,
images, color, focus, announcements, and hover are written out here. The
pieces of the floor that live with their subjects — contrast, tap targets,
focus visibility, enlarged text, reduce motion, a channel that is never touch
or sound alone, a label never replaced by a placeholder — stayed in
rules/states.md, rules/motion.md, and rules/forms.md, and rules/a11y.md names
them rather than moving them.

